## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates mastery of several advanced graph techniques: DAG dynamic programming, multi-criteria optimization, path reconstruction, and cycle handling. It tests the ability to reason about edge attributes with different behaviors (skip-able vs mandatory latency) and implement lexicographic optimization where hops are prioritized over latency.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sorting for efficient DAG processing
- Lexicographic multi-objective optimization (hops first, latency second)
- Predecessor-based path reconstruction
- Cycle detection using DFS and graph condensation
- Edge attribute handling with conditional weight computation

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestPathDAG(graph, s, t) {
  // Assumes graph is a DAG. Each edge has: latency w, skip boolean
  const order = topologicalSort(graph);
  const best = new Map();
  const pred = new Map();

  best.set(s, [0, 0]); // [hops, latency]

  for (const u of order) {
    if (!best.has(u)) continue;
    const [hops, latency] = best.get(u);

    for (const {to: v, latency: w, skip} of graph.edgesFrom(u)) {
      const cand = [hops + 1, latency + (skip ? 0 : w)];
      const current = best.get(v);
      if (!current || lexicographicallySmaller(cand, current)) {
        best.set(v, cand);
        pred.set(v, u);
      }
    }
  }

  return reconstructPath(pred, s, t);
}

function detectCycles(graph) {
  const visited = new Set();
  const recStack = new Set();

  function dfs(u) {
    if (recStack.has(u)) return true; // Cycle detected
    if (visited.has(u)) return false;

    visited.add(u);
    recStack.add(u);

    for (const {to: v} of graph.edgesFrom(u)) {
      if (dfs(v)) return true;
    }

    recStack.delete(u);
    return false;
  }

  return Array.from(graph.nodes()).some(dfs);
}
```

## Adaptation for Cyclic Graphs
If cycles are present, first detect them using DFS with a recursion stack. For graphs with cycles, apply strongly connected components (SCC) decomposition to create a condensation DAG, then solve on the condensed graph. Alternatively, use Dijkstra's algorithm modified for lexicographic optimization where the edge weight is the tuple (1, w or 0 based on skip).

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests fundamental graph algorithms, DAG analysis, and dynamic programming techniques commonly used in project scheduling and dependency management systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Topological Sorting**: Linear ordering respecting task dependencies
- **Dynamic Programming on DAGs**: Computing optimal values through predecessor relationships
- **Earliest Finish Times**: Maximum of predecessor completion plus task duration
- **Makespan Calculation**: Overall project completion time
- **Complexity**: O(N + E) time and space

## Code Example
```javascript
function makespan(n, durations, edges) {
  // Build adjacency list and compute indegrees
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);

  for (const [u, v] of edges) {
    adj[u].push(v);
    indeg[v]++;
  }

  // Initialize earliest finish times
  const earliestFinish = durations.slice();
  const queue = [];

  // Start with source nodes (indegree = 0)
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (indeg[i] === 0) queue.push(i);
  }

  // Process nodes in topological order
  let head = 0;
  while (head < queue.length) {
    const u = queue[head++];

    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      // Update earliest finish for successor
      earliestFinish[v] = Math.max(
        earliestFinish[v],
        earliestFinish[u] + durations[v]
      );

      indeg[v]--;
      if (indeg[v] === 0) queue.push(v);
    }
  }

  // Return makespan (maximum earliest finish time)
  return Math.max(...earliestFinish);
}
```

## Algorithm Insight
The topological approach ensures each node is processed only after all its predecessors are complete, allowing accurate computation of earliest start times and efficient makespan calculation.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to derive a recurrence for a simple constraint (no adjacent picks), then implement both top-down memoization and bottom-up tabulation. Requires correct base cases for N=0 and N=1 and translating to iterative state transitions. Follow-up prompts space optimization and practical coding habits.

## Key Concepts
- DP for non-adjacent max sum (House Robber)
- Top-down memoization with base cases
- Bottom-up tabulation
- Space optimization to O(1)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxNonAdjacent(nums){
  const n = nums.length;
  if (n === 0) return 0;
  const memo = Array(n).fill(-1);
  const solve = i => {
    if (i < 0) return 0;
    if (memo[i] !== -1) return memo[i];
    memo[i] = Math.max(nums[i] + solve(i-2), solve(i-1));
    return memo[i];
  };
  return solve(n-1);
}
function maxNonAdjacentTab(nums){
  const n = nums.length;
  if (n === 0) return 0;
  let prev2 = 0, prev1 = Math.max(0, nums[0]);
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++){
    const cur = Math.max(prev1, nums[i] + prev2);
    prev2 = prev1;
    prev1 = cur;
  }
  return prev1;
}
```

